oo short? From y=168, rising... he'd hit y=160 quickly.\n\nHmm, but coin=0. Something's off.\n\nWait, maybe the ? block in slot 3's frame was at a different x. The screenshot I'm looking at now (after 20 frames) shows a ? block at x~130,y~150. But maybe that's the SECOND ? block, and the FIRST was elsewhere. Ugh, the scrolling confuses.\n\nLet me reconsider: Actually the block we positioned under was found at screen box (121,144)-(137,160). In the current screenshot, the ? block at x~130,y~150 matches. So the block is still there and un-hit (still a ? block). Mario is at x~135, y~185 (on ground). So he jumped but missed or didn't hit.\n\nHmm, why would he miss? If his screen x is 135 now and was 120 before, the scroll must have changed by -15 or Mario moved right 15. But no input. Hmm, maybe the level auto-scrolls? No.\n\nWait, maybe I'm misreading the screenshot: Mario might be at x~128 (his left edge) and the block at x~130-146. Let me not guess; let me read $03AD and $071C now.\n\nActually, the key issue: from slot 3, running 20 frames didn't award a coin. So either the bump didn't happen (position wrong) or the state is bad.\n\nLet me debug by loading slot 2 (on the ground under the block) and doing a jump with play_input [{a,40}] — that worked before (coin awarded). Let me verify slot 2 works. If it does, then the issue is slot 3 (the mid-jump state) — maybe the save during step corrupted the jump, or the bump requires the A-button state.\n\nActually! Important insight: In SMB, the block bump may require the player's \"block bump\" state which is set during the collision. The jump from a mid-air saved state should still collide. But maybe the saved state at frame 1 of the jump had the player in a state where the vertical velocity wasn't stored in the snapshot (e.g., the velocity is derived from the animation/controller). Hmm.\n\nWait, actually the simpler explanation: In SMB, the jump height is controlled by holding A; releasing A early cuts the jump. At slot 3