{"type":"thinking","text":"Now I have the table at $BBF8:\n- $BBF8 = 0x17 (23 decimal) → table1[0] = 0x17\n- $BBF9 = 0x1D (29) → table1[1] = 0x1D\n- $BBFA = 0x0B (11) → table2[0] = 0x0B\n- $BBFB = 0x11 (17) → table2[1] = 0x11\n- $BBFC = 0x02 → sound table?\n- $BBFD = 0x13 (19)\n- $BBFE = 0xA9, 0x01 → lda #$01 (routine start)\n\nSo:\n- `ldy $bbf8,x` with X=$0753. If $0753=0 → Y=0x17; if 1 → Y=0x1D.\n- First add ($BC09): Y=0x17 → target $07D7+(0x17-5)=$07D7+0x12=$07E9 through $07D7+0x17=$07EE. So it writes $07E9-$07EE, with $07EE as the most significant digit! So the first add targets the coin digits ending at $07EE. The coin digits $07ED/$07EE are within $07E9-$07EE.\n\nWait, but the operand is 6 digits $0134-$0139. The routine adds 6 digits. With Y=0x17, the target is $07E9-$07EE (6 bytes). The coin display is $07ED (tens) and $07EE (ones)? Hmm, but the routine treats $07EE as the MOST significant of the 6-digit number. So the \"number\" is $07E9 (ones) ... $07EE (hundred-thousands). That doesn't match a 2-digit coin count at $07ED/$07EE.\n\nHmm, wait. Maybe I have the direction wrong. Let me re-derive: X starts at 5, reads $0134+5 = $0139 (most significant operand digit?). Y starts at Y0=0x17. Stores to $07D7+0x17 = $07EE. Then dey/dex: X=4,$0138; Y=0x16,$07ED. ... X=0,$0134; Y=0x12,$07E9. So yes, operand digit $0139 (index 5) → $07EE. So $07EE is the most significant of the 6-byte target, $07E9 the least.\n\nBut the mapping says coin tens=$07ED, ones=$07EE. If $07EE is the most significant of the 6-digit number $07E9..$07EE, that would be a 6-digit number with ones at $07E9. Contradiction.\n\nHmm, unless the routine's loop direction/meaning is different. Let me re-read the routine carefully:\n\n```\n$8f5f: ad 70 07    lda $0770\n$8f62: c9 00       cmp #$00\n$8f64: f0 16       beq $8f7c\n$8f66: a2 05       ldx #$05\n$8f68: bd 34 01    lda $0134,x\n$8f6b: 18          clc\n$8f6c: 79 d7 07    adc $07d7,y\n$8f6f: 30 16       bmi $8f87\n$8f71: c9 0a       cmp #$0a\n$8f7